  "biography": {
    "plain_text": "Walter Runciman, first Viscount Runciman of Doxford, was a British shipowner and Liberal politician. Educated at Trinity College, Cambridge, he managed the Moor Line and inherited his father's shipping business. His parliamentary career included responsibility for education, agriculture and trade, followed by a diplomatic mission to Czechoslovakia before the Second World War.",
    "confidence": {
      "score": 94,
      "band": "high",
      "reason": "Institutional biography, contemporary reference and Columbia establish formation, family business and public offices."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S1",
      "S2",
      "S3"
    ],
    "word_count": 52,
    "html": "<p>Walter Runciman, first Viscount Runciman of Doxford, was a British shipowner and Liberal politician. Educated at Trinity College, Cambridge, he managed the Moor Line and inherited his father's shipping business. His parliamentary career included responsibility for education, agriculture and trade, followed by a diplomatic mission to Czechoslovakia before the Second World War.</p>\r\n"
  },
  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "Walter Runciman arrived in Prague in 1938 to mediate between the Czechoslovak government and the Sudeten German leadership. He recognised the difficulty of his task, telling American diplomats that even limited progress would be worthwhile. The negotiations concerned demands for autonomy in border regions with mixed populations and competing national loyalties.\n\nThe mission failed as Nazi Germany encouraged confrontation instead of agreement. By September, Runciman acknowledged that he had achieved little beyond keeping negotiations alive. His intervention became part of the sequence leading to the Munich Agreement, which followed later that month. This unsuccessful mediation became a defining episode of his public career.",
    "confidence": {
      "score": 93,
      "band": "high",
      "reason": "US diplomatic dispatches record expectations and acknowledgement of failure. Czech military-history institute explains obstruction and connection to Munich."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S4",
      "S5",
      "S6"
    ],
    "word_count": 103,
    "html": "<p>Walter Runciman arrived in Prague in 1938 to mediate between the Czechoslovak government and the Sudeten German leadership. He recognised the difficulty of his task, telling American diplomats that even limited progress would be worthwhile. The negotiations concerned demands for autonomy in border regions with mixed populations and competing national loyalties.</p>\r\n<p>The mission failed as Nazi Germany encouraged confrontation instead of agreement. By September, Runciman acknowledged that he had achieved little beyond keeping negotiations alive. His intervention became part of the sequence leading to the Munich Agreement, which followed later that month. This unsuccessful mediation became a defining episode of his public career.</p>\r\n"
  },
